</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yu Zhao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Beijing Institute of Technology, Haidian, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:zy199402@live.com">zy199402@live.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth projective surface over <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under certain technical assumptions, we prove that the degeneracy locus of the universal sheaf over the moduli space of stable sheaves is either empty or an irreducible Cohen–Macaulay variety of the expected dimension; we also give a criterion for when the degeneracy locus is nonempty. This result generalizes the work of Bayer, Chen, and Jiang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for the Hilbert scheme of points on surfaces.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">The above statement is a special case of a more general phenomenon: for a two-term complex of locally free sheaves, the geometry of the degeneracy locus is closely related to the geometry of Grassmannians.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a pair <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,N</annotation></semantics></math> of moduli spaces of sheaves or complexes over a smooth variety, a fundamental problem is to study the Brill–Noether loci in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math> determined by the dimension of the space of morphisms between the corresponding objects. When <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex algebraic surface <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\cong S^{[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\cong S^{[n]}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hilbert scheme of ideals <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset\mathcal{O}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset\mathcal{O}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of colength <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th Brill–Noether locus is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(I,x)\in S^{[n]}\times S\mid\dim\mathrm{Hom}(I,\mathbb{C}_{x})\geq l+1\},\quad l\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(I,x)\in S^{[n]}\times S\mid\dim\mathrm{Hom}(I,\mathbb{C}_{x})\geq l+1\},\quad l\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is the same as the <span id="S1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">degeneracy locus</span> of the universal ideal sheaf</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BN}_{l,n}:=\{(I,x)\in S^{[n]}\times S\mid\mathrm{rank}(I|_{x})\geq l+1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BN</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BN}_{l,n}:=\{(I,x)\in S^{[n]}\times S\mid\mathrm{rank}(I|_{x})\geq l+1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">by Nakayama’s lemma. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Bayer, Chen, and Jiang proved that <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BN}_{l,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BN</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BN}_{l,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible Cohen–Macaulay variety of dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+2-l(l+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+2-l(l+1)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-l(1+l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-l(1+l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and is otherwise empty. The Hilbert scheme of points on a surface can be viewed as the moduli space of stable sheaves of rank <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we generalize the above result to the moduli space of stable sheaves of all ranks <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We describe our main result through the degeneracy locus of the universal sheaf: given a coherent sheaf <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> over a variety <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the degeneracy locus <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{F},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{F},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the closed subvariety</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{F},l}:=\{p\in X\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathcal{F}|_{p})\geq l\},\quad l\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{F},l}:=\{p\in X\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathcal{F}|_{p})\geq l\},\quad l\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We fix <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}\in\mathrm{NS}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}\in\mathrm{NS}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> and an ample line bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in H^{4}(S,\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in H^{4}(S,\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli space of Gieseker <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-stable coherent sheaves with rank <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, first Chern class <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and second Chern class <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal sheaf over <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We consider the following assumptions, which hold for a generic ample divisor when the surface <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a del Pezzo, K3, or abelian surface:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gcd(r,c_{1}\cdot H)=1,\qquad K_{S}\cong\mathcal{O}_{S}\text{ or }K_{S}\cdot H&lt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gcd(r,c_{1}\cdot H)=1,\qquad K_{S}\cong\mathcal{O}_{S}\text{ or }K_{S}\cdot H&lt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the canonical line bundle of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> holds, then we have</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that for any </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nonempty if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l):=n-n_{0}-\frac{l(l-r)-t(t-r)}{2r}\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l):=n-n_{0}-\frac{l(l-r)-t(t-r)}{2r}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the remainder of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> divided by </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In particular, </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nonempty if and only if </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:=(1-r)c_{1}^{2}-(r^{2}-1)\chi(\mathcal{O}_{S})+h^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:=(1-r)c_{1}^{2}-(r^{2}-1)\chi(\mathcal{O}_{S})+h^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{S})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. If </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the degeneracy locus </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an irreducible Cohen-Macaulay variety of dimension</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vdim}_{n,l}:=\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}+2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>vdim</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vdim}_{n,l}:=\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}+2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> (and our proof) can be generalized to the case <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)=n-\frac{l(l-1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)=n-\frac{l(l-1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is exactly the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. When <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, Yoshioka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=\lfloor\tfrac{-\beta}{2r}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=\lfloor\tfrac{-\beta}{2r}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> for a K3 surface with a generic ample divisor, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=\lfloor\tfrac{2-\beta}{2r}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=\lfloor\tfrac{2-\beta}{2r}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> for an abelian surface. When <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Theorem B of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. An upper bound for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> was given by Li–Qin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have an argument similar to <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> for the moduli space of framed sheaves on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. It is summarized in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Moduli Space of Framed Coherent Sheaves on the Plane and ADHM Construction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Different from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we prove a much more general argument and apply it to the universal sheaf. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible, locally complete intersection variety over an algebraically closed field, and consider a morphism of locally free sheaves over <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\xrightarrow{\sigma}W\text{ with its dual }\sigma^{\vee}:W^{\vee}\to V^{\vee}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜎</mo></mover><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> with its dual </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\xrightarrow{\sigma}W\text{ with its dual }\sigma^{\vee}:W^{\vee}\to V^{\vee}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given any <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th degeneracy locus <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}:=\overline{D}_{\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}:=\overline{D}_{\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l}</annotation></semantics></math> as the closed subvariety consisting of points <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{x}))\leq l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{x}))\leq l</annotation></semantics></math>. We also consider the Grassmannian (in Grothendieck’s notation) <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l):=\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l):=\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l)</annotation></semantics></math>, which parametrizes the quotients of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{coker}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{coker}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> by locally free sheaves of rank <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:=\mathrm{rank}(W)-\mathrm{rank}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:=\mathrm{rank}(W)-\mathrm{rank}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we prove that</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, either <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)</annotation></semantics></math>) is empty, or <a href="#S1.E2" title="In Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> (resp. <a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, <a href="#S1.E4" title="In Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) holds:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,l})\geq\dim(X)-\max\{0,l(l-e)\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,l})\geq\dim(X)-\max\{0,l(l-e)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l-e)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l-e),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l+e)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l+e).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If one of the above three inequalities is an equality for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the other two inequalities are equalities for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and moreover</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the variety </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is empty (resp. irreducible) if and only if </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is empty (resp. irreducible).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The degeneracy locus </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is irreducible and Cohen-Macaulay for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{-},l_{+}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{-},l_{+}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the incidence variety</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+}):=\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma^{\vee}),l_{-})\times_{X}\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l_{+})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+}):=\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma^{\vee}),l_{-})\times_{X}\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma),l_{+}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a locally complete intersection variety of dimension</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{l_{-},l_{+}}=\mathrm{dim}(X)-l_{-}(l_{-}+e)-l_{+}(l_{+}-e)+l_{-}l_{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{l_{-},l_{+}}=\mathrm{dim}(X)-l_{-}(l_{-}+e)-l_{+}(l_{+}-e)+l_{-}l_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the language of <span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">derived algebraic geometry</span>, the Grassmannian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,d)</annotation></semantics></math> and the incidence variety <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> can be endowed with a derived structure, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Thus, in the language of derived algebraic geometry, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> states that if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> is classical for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> are also classical for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l,l_{-},l_{+}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l,l_{-},l_{+}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Our proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> also relies on computing the virtual dimensions of these derived schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note a difference in notation compared to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l)</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee}[1],l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee}[1],l)</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason is that we regard <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism of locally free sheaves instead of a two-term complex. <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> can also be generalized to the case where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a two-term complex of locally free sheaves (locally on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>) without difficulty.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, if <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-smooth variety over <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is also quasi-smooth, which induces a perfect obstruction theory in the sense of Li-Tian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> or Behrend-Fantechi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, thereby inducing a virtual fundamental class</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]^{\mathrm{vir}}\in\mathrm{CH}_{d_{l_{-},l_{+}}}(\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>vir</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CH</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]^{\mathrm{vir}}\in\mathrm{CH}_{d_{l_{-},l_{+}}}(\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Now we assume that all the inequalities in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> are equalities. Then any incidence variety <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally complete intersection variety of dimension <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby recovering an earlier argument by the author in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 4.6 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]^{\mathrm{vir}}=[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]\in\mathrm{CH}_{d_{0}}(\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>vir</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CH</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]^{\mathrm{vir}}=[\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})]\in\mathrm{CH}_{d_{0}}(\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Notations</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we assume that the dimension of the empty set is an arbitrary integer, so that any equalities and inequalities involving the dimension of a variety continue to hold even when the variety is empty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Acknowledgement</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While preparing this paper, the author was reminded by Qingyuan Jiang that a similar argument to <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> was given in his note <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The author would like to thank Qingyuan Jiang for many helpful discussions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonempty locally complete intersection variety over an algebraically closed field, and let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:V\to W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:V\to W</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of locally free sheaves over <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:=\mathrm{rank}(W)-\mathrm{rank}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:=\mathrm{rank}(W)-\mathrm{rank}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\vee}:W^{\vee}\to V^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\vee}:W^{\vee}\to V^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual of <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Section 14.4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th degeneracy locus as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}:=\{x\in X\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{k(x)}))\geq l\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}:=\{x\in X\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{k(x)}))\geq l\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is a closed subvariety of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the residue field of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We also define the locally closed subvariety</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,l}:=\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}-\overline{D}_{\sigma,l+1}=\{x\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{k(x)}))=l\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,l}:=\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}-\overline{D}_{\sigma,l+1}=\{x\mid\mathrm{rank}(\mathrm{coker}(\sigma|_{k(x)}))=l\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By the definition of degeneracy loci, we have <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}=\overline{D}_{\sigma^{\vee},k-e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}=\overline{D}_{\sigma^{\vee},k-e}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}\cong X\quad\mathrm{if}\quad k\leq\max\{0,e\},\quad D_{\sigma,k}\cong\emptyset\quad\mathrm{if}\quad k&lt;\max\{0,e\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>if</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>if</mi><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}\cong X\quad\mathrm{if}\quad k\leq\max\{0,e\},\quad D_{\sigma,k}\cong\emptyset\quad\mathrm{if}\quad k&lt;\max\{0,e\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem 14.4 (b)(c) of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,k})\geq\dim(X)-k(k-e)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,k})\geq\dim(X)-k(k-e).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, if the inequality is an equality, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math>) is Cohen-Macaulay (resp. a locally complete intersection variety).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l-e),\quad\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l+e)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l-e),\quad\dim(\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma^{\vee},l))\geq\dim(X)-l(l+e).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, the first inequality of <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is an equality for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the second inequality is an equality for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also equivalent to the condition</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,l})=\dim(X)-l(l-e),\text{ for all }l\geq\max\{0,e\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma,l})=\dim(X)-l(l-e),\text{ for all }l\geq\max\{0,e\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the above equivalent conditions hold, for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty (resp. irreducible) if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> is empty (resp. irreducible).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We only prove the inequalities about <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math>, as the rank of <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-e</annotation></semantics></math> and we can apply the same argument to <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed with the structure of a quasi-smooth derived scheme and its virtual dimension is <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)+l(e-l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)+l(e-l)</annotation></semantics></math>. As the dimension of a quasi-smooth scheme is always greater than or equal to its virtual dimension, we have <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now assume that the first inequality of <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is an equality for all <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection from <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the fiber over <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}(k,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}(k,l)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle; in particular, over <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber is <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}(l,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}(l,l)</annotation></semantics></math> (a point). Thus, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(D_{\sigma,l})\leq\dim(X)-l(l-e)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(D_{\sigma,l})\leq\dim(X)-l(l-e)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math> if it is not empty.
As <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)-l(l-e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)-l(l-e)</annotation></semantics></math> is a strictly decreasing function of <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next we assume that <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> holds for all <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We notice that the fiber of <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty when <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;\max\{l,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;\max\{l,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and has dimension</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l(k-l)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l(k-l)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is a strictly decreasing function of <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq\max\{l,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq\max\{l,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence it is achieved at the maximal value at <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=e</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l</annotation></semantics></math>, which are both <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)+l(e-l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)+l(e-l)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the inequalities in <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> are equalities for all <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we notice that <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> are both nonempty when <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq l\leq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq l\leq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now assume that <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> holds for all <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then when <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, as</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l(k-l),\quad k\geq\max\{l,e\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l(k-l),\quad k\geq\max\{l,e\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">achieves its maximal value at <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l</annotation></semantics></math>, the union of fibers over <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;l</annotation></semantics></math> has strictly smaller dimension than <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)+l(e-l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)+l(e-l)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> is also dense in <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{X}(\sigma,l)</annotation></semantics></math> is empty if and only if <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma,l}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove that if <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Degeneracy Theory for Two-term Complexes ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> holds for all <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq\max\{0,e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq\max\{0,e\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally complete intersection variety of dimension</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=\dim(X)-l_{-}(l_{-}+e)-l_{+}(l_{+}-e)+l_{-}l_{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=\dim(X)-l_{-}(l_{-}+e)-l_{+}(l_{+}-e)+l_{-}l_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">The reason is that its fiber over <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty if <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and its dimension is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l_{-}(k-e-l_{-})+l_{+}(k-l_{+})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)-k(k-e)+l_{-}(k-e-l_{-})+l_{+}(k-l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">which is strictly decreasing when <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the maximal value is achieved at <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\max\{l_{-}+e,l_{+}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is exactly <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The irreducibility of <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Inc</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Inc}_{X}(\sigma,l_{-},l_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the fact that it is endowed with a quasi-smooth derived scheme structure in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and its virtual dimension is also <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix A of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> for more details).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Degeneracy Loci on the Smooth Moduli Surface of Coherent Sheaves over a Surface</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. We always assume <a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> for the remainder of this section. We first recall basic facts about the moduli space of stable sheaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a unique integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the Bogomolov inequality, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math> is empty for <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;\tfrac{1}{4}c_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;\tfrac{1}{4}c_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, by Theorem 1.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, there exists a constant <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty for all <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there exists a smallest integer <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty. Let <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be a stable sheaf. Set <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{n_{0}}:=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{n_{0}}:=U</annotation></semantics></math>, and inductively construct a sequence of coherent sheaves <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{n_{0}}\supset U_{n_{0}+1}\supset U_{n_{0}+2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{n_{0}}\supset U_{n_{0}+1}\supset U_{n_{0}+2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k}/U_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k}/U_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a length-1 skyscraper sheaf for all <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition 5.5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, all <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are stable coherent sheaves.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 2.10, 2.14 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and Lemma 3.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a smooth projective variety of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+2rn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+2rn</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the universal sheaf <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has a resolution of the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{V}_{n}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{n}}\mathcal{W}_{n}\to\mathcal{U}_{n}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">n</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{V}_{n}\xrightarrow{\sigma_{n}}\mathcal{W}_{n}\to\mathcal{U}_{n}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism between the locally free sheaves <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a closed point <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n,o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n,o}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times\{o\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)\times\{o\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Proposition 2.10, 2.14 of [] and Lemma 3.3 of []). ‣ 3. Degeneracy Loci on the Smooth Moduli Surface of Coherent Sheaves over a Surface ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given integers <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l,n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n)}(\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n-l)}(\sigma_{n-l,o},l)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n)}(\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(r,c_{1},n-l)}(\sigma_{n-l,o},l),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is either empty or a smooth variety of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+2rn-l(l+r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+2rn-l(l+r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is actually a corollary of the study of stable perverse coherent sheaves on blowups of surfaces by Nakajima–Yoshioka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}:=\mathrm{Bl}_{o}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Bl</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}:=\mathrm{Bl}_{o}S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\hat{S}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\hat{S}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection map. Let <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:=p^{-1}(o)\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:=p^{-1}(o)\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the exceptional divisor and <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1):=\mathcal{O}(C)|_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1):=\mathcal{O}(C)|_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]\in H^{2}(\hat{S},\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]\in H^{2}(\hat{S},\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> be the fundamental class of <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">A stable perverse coherent sheaf <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a coherent sheaf such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}(E,\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1))=0,\quad\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal{O}_{C},E)=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}(E,\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1))=0,\quad\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal{O}_{C},E)=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">and <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}E</annotation></semantics></math> is Gieseker stable with respect to <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,l\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,l\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{0}(n,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{0}(n,l)</annotation></semantics></math> as the moduli space of perverse coherent sheaves with the total Chern class</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n,l}:=(r,p^{*}c_{1}-l[C],n-\frac{l^{2}+l}{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n,l}:=(r,p^{*}c_{1}-l[C],n-\frac{l^{2}+l}{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Its corresponding Chern character is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}(r,c_{1},\frac{1}{2}c_{1}^{2}-n)-l(ch(\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1)))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}(r,c_{1},\frac{1}{2}c_{1}^{2}-n)-l(ch(\mathcal{O}_{C}(-1))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem 4.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> (see also Theorem 4.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{0}(n,l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(c)}(\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l),\quad M^{0}(n,l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(c-l[pts])}(\sigma_{n-l,o},l)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{0}(n,l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(c)}(\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l),\quad M^{0}(n,l)\cong\mathrm{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}^{s}_{H}(c-l[pts])}(\sigma_{n-l,o},l).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma 3.22 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> (see also Lemma 3.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>), <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{0}(n,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{0}(n,l)</annotation></semantics></math> is either empty or a smooth variety of dimension <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+2rn-l(l+r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+2rn-l(l+r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we finish the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3. Degeneracy Loci on the Smooth Moduli Surface of Coherent Sheaves over a Surface ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We choose a closed point <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. We first prove that <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty if and only if <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq r</annotation></semantics></math>, we notice that by <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3. Degeneracy Loci on the Smooth Moduli Surface of Coherent Sheaves over a Surface ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
<math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}=\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}=\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o}^{\vee},l-r}</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty
if and only if <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n-l+r,o},l-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n-l+r,o},l-r}</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty. When <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq r</annotation></semantics></math>, we also have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)=g(n-l+r,l-r)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)=g(n-l+r,l-r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus we can reduce to the case that <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq l&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq l&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)=n-n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)=n-n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}=\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}=\mathcal{M}_{H}^{s}(r,c_{1},n)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is nonempty if and only if <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-n_{0}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-n_{0}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we prove the argument.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we prove the argument that <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible Cohen-Macaulay variety of dimension <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. It also directly follows from <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3. Degeneracy Loci on the Smooth Moduli Surface of Coherent Sheaves over a Surface ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we consider <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}=\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}=\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math>. We know that <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty if and only if <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty for some closed point <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence it is equivalent to <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now assume that <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Regarding <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math> as a scheme over <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, for any closed point <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, its fiber is <math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n,o},l}</annotation></semantics></math> and has dimension <math id="S3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l})=\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}+2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\overline{D}_{\sigma_{n},l})=\beta+2rn-\max\{0,l(l-r)\}+2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, we finish the proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Moduli Space of Framed Coherent Sheaves on the Plane and ADHM Construction</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given integers <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,n)\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,n)\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}\times\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli space of framed sheaves on the projective plane. It can also be interpreted as a Nakajima quiver variety: we consider two vector spaces <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(V)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(V)=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(W)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(W)=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The ADHM datum for this setup is given by</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(X,Y,i,j)\in\operatorname{Hom}(V,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(W,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(X,Y,i,j)\in\operatorname{Hom}(V,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(W,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">subject to the equation</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X,Y]+ij=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X,Y]+ij=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">An ADHM datum <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y,i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y,i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is called stable if there is no non-zero subspace <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> closed under <math id="A1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and contained in the kernel of <math id="A1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of stable ADHM data. The general linear group <math id="A1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{GL}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{GL}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="A1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\cdot(X,Y,i,j)=(gXg^{-1},gYg^{-1},gi,jg^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\cdot(X,Y,i,j)=(gXg^{-1},gYg^{-1},gi,jg^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chapter 2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\cong\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)/\mathrm{GL}(V)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\cong\mathrm{M}^{s}(V,W)/\mathrm{GL}(V),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-empty smooth variety of dimension <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2rn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2rn</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The vector spaces <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> induce tautological locally free sheaves on <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the two-term complex</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{n}\xrightarrow{(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus j}\mathcal{V}_{n}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{n}\oplus\mathcal{W}\xrightarrow{(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus-(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus i}\mathcal{V}_{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">⊕</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">⊕</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">⊕</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi></mrow></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{n}\xrightarrow{(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus j}\mathcal{V}_{n}\oplus\mathcal{V}_{n}\oplus\mathcal{W}\xrightarrow{(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus-(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus i}\mathcal{V}_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbb{A}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbb{A}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the map</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus j" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>id</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X-x\cdot\text{id})\oplus(Y-y\cdot\text{id})\oplus j</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is injective at every closed point, its cokernel, denoted by <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is locally free. Moreover, we have a morphism of locally free sheaves</p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}:\mathcal{V}_{n}\to\mathcal{T}_{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}:\mathcal{V}_{n}\to\mathcal{T}_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the (virtual) rank of <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-r</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal sheaf <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\mathbb{A}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\mathbb{A}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}\cong\mathrm{coker}(\sigma_{n}^{\vee})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}\cong\mathrm{coker}(\sigma_{n}^{\vee}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n,0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the restriction of <math id="A1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\{(0,0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\{(0,0)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by setting <math id="A1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=y=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Grassmannian of <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> also has a quiver-like description due to
Nakajima–Yoshioka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be vector spaces of dimensions <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We consider the datum <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{1},B_{2},d,i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{1},B_{2},d,i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}(V_{1},V_{2})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V_{2},V_{1})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(W,V_{1})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V_{2},W)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}(V_{1},V_{2})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V_{2},V_{1})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(W,V_{1})\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V_{2},W),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying the equation</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}dB_{2}-B_{2}dB_{1}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}dB_{2}-B_{2}dB_{1}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">A datum <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{1},B_{2},d,i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{1},B_{2},d,i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is called stable if for any subspaces</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}^{\prime}\subset V_{1},\quad V_{2}^{\prime}\subset V_{2},\quad 0\subset W" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}^{\prime}\subset V_{1},\quad V_{2}^{\prime}\subset V_{2},\quad 0\subset W</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">which are closed under the quiver representation, we must have <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By the stability condition, <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, implying <math id="A1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\geq n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\geq n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{M}^{s}(V_{1},V_{2},W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{M}^{s}(V_{1},V_{2},W)</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of stable data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nakajima-Yoshioka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli space</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{perv}}(n_{1},n_{2},r):=\mathrm{M}^{s}(V_{1},V_{2},W)/(\mathrm{GL}(V_{1})\times\mathrm{GL}(V_{2}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">perv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{perv}}(n_{1},n_{2},r):=\mathrm{M}^{s}(V_{1},V_{2},W)/(\mathrm{GL}(V_{1})\times\mathrm{GL}(V_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is either an irreducible smooth variety of dimension <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}n_{2}-n_{1}^{2}-n_{2}^{2}+(n_{1}+n_{2})r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}n_{2}-n_{1}^{2}-n_{2}^{2}+(n_{1}+n_{2})r</annotation></semantics></math> or an empty set. Moreover, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}_{fr}(n_{2},r)}(\sigma_{n_{2},0}^{\vee},n_{1}-n_{2})\cong\mathcal{M}_{\text{perv}}(n_{1},n_{2},r)\cong\operatorname{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}_{fr}(n_{1},r)}(\sigma_{n_{1},0},n_{1}-n_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">perv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}_{fr}(n_{2},r)}(\sigma_{n_{2},0}^{\vee},n_{1}-n_{2})\cong\mathcal{M}_{\text{perv}}(n_{1},n_{2},r)\cong\operatorname{Gr}_{\mathcal{M}_{fr}(n_{1},r)}(\sigma_{n_{1},0},n_{1}-n_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.2. The Degeneracy Locus and the Grassmannian ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we conclude:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any integers <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the degeneracy locus <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\mathbb{A}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{fr}(r,n)\times\mathbb{A}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty if and only if</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}(n,l):=n-\frac{l(l-r)-t(t-r)}{2r}\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}(n,l):=n-\frac{l(l-r)-t(t-r)}{2r}\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the remainder when <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is divided by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}(n,l)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}(n,l)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D}_{\mathcal{U}_{n},l}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible Cohen–Macaulay variety of dimension</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2rn+2-\max\{0,l(r-l)-t(r-t)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2rn+2-\max\{0,l(r-l)-t(r-t)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is the same as the proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1.1. The Brill-Noether theory on the Moduli Space of Stable Sheaves ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The Degeneracy Loci for Smooth Moduli of Sheaves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, which we omit here.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
